-- Blog: how to profit selling trading card lots (2026-11-08).
INSERT INTO blog_posts (slug, title, description, keyword, intro, body, status, published_at) VALUES
('how-to-profit-selling-trading-card-lots', 'How to Profit Selling Trading Card Lots (and Keep Your Collection a Manageable Size)', 'How to make money selling sports and TCG card lots on Slabs & Singles: building lots buyers want, pricing them, keeping fees and shipping low, and using lots to keep your collection under control.', 'selling trading card lots', 'Every collector ends up with the same problem: boxes of cards that are worth something together but almost nothing one at a time. Listing a 50¢ common on its own costs more in time, fees and postage than it earns, so the boxes keep growing. Lots fix that. Grouping cards into one listing turns a pile you can''t sell into a sale you can, and it''s the easiest way to keep your collection from taking over the house. Here''s how to build lots buyers want, price them so you actually profit, and sell them on Slabs & Singles.', '[{"h": "Why lots make money"}, {"p": "A lot is one listing that sells several cards together for one price. The cards inside don''t have to be valuable on their own. What makes a lot profitable is that every cost you''d pay per sale is paid once instead of over and over:"}, {"list": ["One listing to photograph and write, instead of dozens.", "One marketplace fee: on Slabs & Singles a lot counts as one item sold, however many cards are in it.", "One payment processing charge and one package to ship.", "One buyer to message, pack for and get feedback from."]}, {"h": "The math: 25 singles vs. one lot"}, {"p": "Say you have 25 cards worth about $1 each. Sold one at a time to 25 different buyers, each sale has its own costs:"}, {"list": ["Stripe''s card processing is usually 2.9% + 30¢ per order. The 30¢ alone is $7.50 across 25 orders.", "Each card ships separately: 25 envelopes at 82¢ a stamp is $20.50 in postage, before sleeves, toploaders and envelopes.", "Our fee applies to each of the 25 sales ({fee.flat} per card up to {fee.threshold}).", "And you list, pack and ship 25 times."]}, {"p": "Sold as one 25-card lot for $25, you pay one processing charge (about $1.03), one marketplace fee and one label. A lot this size usually ships in a bubble mailer or small box for about $5–7 by USPS Ground Advantage. Most of what''s left is yours, for one listing''s worth of work."}, {"note": "Lots usually sell for less per card than the cards would bring one by one, but far more than they''d ever net individually. The goal is the most money for the least effort, not the highest price per card."}, {"h": "What makes a lot sell"}, {"p": "A random stack is hard to sell. A lot with a clear theme sells itself, because the buyer can tell at a glance whether it''s for them. Lots that do well:"}, {"list": ["Player lots: 10, 25 or 50 cards of one player, often across years and sets. Player collectors buy these constantly.", "Team lots: one team, one era or all-time. Great for fans and team collectors.", "Rookie lots: rookie cards (RC) from one year or one sport. Collectors buy these hoping one breaks out.", "Set lots: a run of cards from one set toward completing it, with the card numbers listed.", "Insert and parallel lots: a stack of shiny cards (refractors, prizms, holos) sells better than the same number of base cards.", "TCG lots: a stack of holos or reverse holos, a playset of a deck''s commons, or bulk rares from one set.", "Starter lots: an assortment of 100 or more mixed cards priced for kids and new collectors. These move slower stock fast.", "Mixed-sport lots: fine for true bulk. List them under Multi-Sport."]}, {"h": "Pull the hits first"}, {"p": "Before a card goes in a lot, check whether it''s worth more on its own. As a rule of thumb, anything that would sell for a few dollars or more by itself, such as stars, key rookies, numbered parallels, autographs and anything graded, is usually better as a single listing. Lots are for the cards that aren''t worth the effort alone."}, {"h": "How to price a lot"}, {"list": ["Search for recently sold lots like yours (same player, team, sport or game, and similar size), not asking prices.", "Think in price per card. A 50-card base lot might bring 10–25¢ a card; a lot of 10 parallels or rookies of a popular player can bring much more.", "Build in shipping. Every listing ships free on Slabs & Singles, so the price must cover your box or mailer and the label. A bigger lot means a heavier package.", "Mention one or two of the best cards in the title, for example “25-card Patrick Mahomes lot with 2 rookies”. That''s what searchers type.", "Turn on Best Offer if you''re not sure. Lot buyers like to negotiate, and an offer tells you what the market thinks."]}, {"h": "Photos and description"}, {"list": ["Lay the cards out in rows and photograph the fronts so every card is visible. Add close-ups of the best cards and of any damage.", "You can add up to 12 photos. For big lots, show the stack and a few rows that represent it honestly.", "In the description, list the notable cards (player, year, set, number) and give the general condition, for example “mostly near mint, a few with soft corners”.", "Say exactly how many cards are included. The “Lot of 25” badge comes from the number you enter."]}, {"h": "How to list a lot on Slabs & Singles"}, {"list": ["Go to Sell and choose List a lot (or choose Lot on the List a card form).", "Enter the number of cards (2 to 5,000), a clear title, the sport or game, a set (or “Mixed”) and the condition of most of the cards.", "Add your photos and description.", "Set your price. A lot counts as one listing for your seller level''s price limit.", "Leave the package as a bubble mailer or box. We estimate the weight from the card count, and you can change it."]}, {"p": "Buyers find lots with the Singles or lots filter in search, and every lot shows a “Lot of” badge in results, so lot shoppers can spot yours quickly."}, {"links": [{"label": "Selling lots", "href": "/help/lots", "text": "the full step-by-step guide."}, {"label": "Lots for sale now", "href": "/search?lots=only", "text": "see what other sellers are listing."}]}, {"h": "Shipping a lot"}, {"list": ["Sleeve the best cards. Stack the rest in team bags or wrap them in a penny-sleeve-sized bundle so they can''t slide.", "Use a bubble mailer for small lots and a box for big or valuable ones. Fill empty space so nothing moves.", "Lots always ship with a tracked label. When you buy the label from the order, pick your actual box (bubble mailer, small, medium or large box, or a custom size) and enter the packed weight in pounds and ounces, then choose Update rates. Weigh the sealed package if you can, because carriers charge the difference if it''s heavier than the label says.", "Ship within 3 business days of the sale."]}, {"links": [{"label": "Buying and printing shipping labels", "href": "/help/shipping-labels"}, {"label": "Packing guide", "href": "/help/packing"}]}, {"h": "Use lots to keep your collection under control"}, {"p": "Selling lots isn''t just about profit. It''s the easiest way to stop a collection from outgrowing your shelves. A simple system that works:"}, {"list": ["Sort every new box into four piles: keep, sell as a single, sell in a lot, and give away.", "Set a value line for singles, for example $3. Anything under the line that you don''t love goes to the lot pile. Revisit the line as prices change.", "Keep lot piles by theme (one box per team, player or game) so lots build themselves as you sort.", "When a theme box reaches 25 or 50 cards, list it. Don''t wait for the perfect lot.", "Pick a regular lot day, such as once a month, to photograph and list whatever is ready.", "Try one in, one out: when you add a new box to your collection, list a lot from an old one.", "Give away true bulk you can''t sell, such as duplicate commons, to kids, card shops, schools or charities. Space is worth something too."]}, {"h": "Let FireCardManager do the bookkeeping"}, {"p": "If you track your cards in FireCardManager, its Lots page lets you build a lot from photos of the stack, set the card count, price, weight and box, and list it on Slabs & Singles or eBay in one click. When it sells on Slabs & Singles, the next order refresh marks the lot sold, adds it to your Sold Log and ends the eBay listing so it can''t sell twice. Virtual Storage Boxes show what you have left in each box, so you know which piles are ready to become the next lot."}, {"links": [{"label": "Connect FireCardManager", "href": "/help/firecardmanager"}]}, {"h": "Quick checklist"}, {"list": ["Hits pulled and listed as singles.", "Lot built around one clear theme.", "Title names the theme, the card count and the best cards.", "Price checked against sold lots, with shipping built in.", "Every card visible in the photos, and notable cards listed in the description.", "Shipped in the right box, with the real weight entered on the label."]}, {"links": [{"label": "List a lot", "href": "/sell/new?lot=1"}, {"label": "Start selling", "href": "/sell"}, {"label": "Selling fees", "href": "/help/fees"}]}]'::jsonb, 'published', '2026-11-08T22:40:00Z')
ON CONFLICT (slug) DO NOTHING;
